  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 409 AM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nThe pattern progression in the short term is a complex transition of \ndeep, split-stream troughing over the Plains with embedded shortwaves \nwhich becomes a closed low centered over southern MN by Wednesday. \nAt the sfc, a low pressure over MN lifts northeast over western Lake \nSuperior today, followed by a CO low lifting toward the northwest \nMN/WI state line by Wednesday morning where it phases with remnant \nlow pressure. From there the models begin to struggle resolving the \nclosed low and resulting progression of the sfc low, but the general \nconsensus is the mid level ridge over the northern Rockies \neventually shifts this eastward as it opens up on Thursday. This \ntrough lingers over the Great Lakes through Saturday before quickly \nbeing followed up by a shortwave from the Canadian Prairie. \n\nA dry slot reaching over the west yields a lull in the showers this \nmorning, isolated to scattered shower coverage in the east lingers \nbehind the low lifting northeast over western Lake MI. But, an \nembedded shortwave kicks off another round of showers early this \nafternoon. Then lingering weaker low pressure over MN is further \nreinforced tonight as a CO low lifts into the region. As this low \nmeanders through Wisconsin tomorrow, it will keep the U.P. in the \nwarm sector, thus resulting in another day of unseasonably high \ntemperatures in the 60s. Simultaneously, on-and-off rain showers \nwill persist through mid-week with highest rainfall chances \nconcentrated in the Wed 06-18Z time frame. Initially, highest \nrainfall amounts up to 0.50\" will be focused over the southern \nportions of the U.P., but as this expands northward into the \nKeweenaw, widespread rain totals of 1 to 2\" will be likely over both \nthat southern sector and northern Keweenaw. In addition, with some \nweak instability, a few rumbles of thunder also cannot be ruled out. \nDue to the lingering drought, though, a flood threat is not \nanticipated.\n  \nAs the aforementioned low continues its eastward track through the \nend of the week, model guidance continues to hint at a split with \nthe northerly low going into northern Quebec and the remnant low \nremaining over the Great Lakes. This remnant low will be responsible \nfor lake enhanced and eventually pure lake effect precipitation over \nthe north to northeast wind belts early Thursday into early Friday. \nAnd, although precipitation will mainly be in the form of rain, \nthere could start to be a mix with some snowfall for the first time \nthis season in the trailing CAA. Further out, drier weather will \nreturn for the weekend as high pressure strengthens over the area, \nand once again there will be a slight warmup into the 50s after a \nbrief dip into the 40s for daytime highs on Friday. However, \nsignificant nighttime radiational cooling will allow overnight lows \nto drop into the 20s.",
